\f1\fs22 Sarah lay on the examination table, her hand unconsciously protective over her belly as Kathy prepared the ultrasound. The room felt more like the inside of a toy box, perhaps because of the additional monitoring equipment Kathy had crammed into it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Everything feeling normal?\'94 Kathy asked.\
\'93Nothing about this is normal,\'94 Sarah said with the tone she reserved for asking good friends to up their game.\
Kathy nodded, spreading gel across Sarah\'92s abdomen. \'93Let\'92s see what Little Clinton\'92s up to today.\'94 She applied the ultrasound\'92s transducer and circled it around a little while she studied its screen. Her shoulders eased a fraction. \'93Well. He looks normal.\'94 She set down the transducer and wiped the gel off Sarah\'92s belly.\
Kathy helped Sarah sit up and caught the troubled set of her mouth. \'93Are you feeling okay?\'94 Sarah looked healthy enough, but that wasn\'92t the same thing as feeling right.\
Sarah blinked, and took her time. \'93And this\'85 well, it\'92s stupid.\'94\
Kathy gave her an attentive stare and the time to arrange her thoughts.\
\'93Well, I\'92ve never been sick a day in my life, at least since I was seven or eight years old.\'94 Sarah shook her head. Those days were hauntingly dim for her. \'93Even so, I feel even healthier than I\'92ve ever been.\'94\
\'93And that\'92s your complaint?\'94 Kathy was only moderately trying to make light of it.\
\'93I know, it\'92s stupid. But I feel like there\'92s something magical about Little Clinton.\'94\
\'93Spoken like every mother ever.\'94\
\'93No. I mean\'85\'94 Sarah searched for the foundations of how she felt. \'93It\'92s like he\'92s the one nurturing me. No, it\'92s more than that. It\'92s like he knows what I need.\'94\
Kathy felt a sharp need for a patch at that point. Quite suddenly, she missed Chanta. \'93You know, Chanta kept saying Little Clinton was the influence for all his songwriting.\'94\
Sarah snorted in a perfectly feminine manner. \'93Well, I have not known Mr. Fairy Boxers for long, but it seems the deepest thing about him is his apparent shallowness.\'94\
Kathy slapped Sarah\'92s wrist. \'93Don\'92t you go dissing my old mate Chanta. There\'92s not a devious bone in his body. Well, except for his bone, which bananas extravagantly to the right.\'94\
\'93I would know nothing about that,\'94 Sarah said sternly.\
Kathy smiled. \'93You must admit, he\'92s always been so attentive toward the little fella. In the beginning, I thought he was just obsessed with you, and to be frank, who wouldn\'92t be?\'94 Kathy pulled out of that spiral of thoughts by picturing Hayley. \'93But it\'92s more than that, he\'92s obsessed with both of you.\'94\
Sarah grew impatient. \'93I had no say in this whatsoever. I told you, those sage gnomes impregnated me.\'94 Didn\'92t they? She still had her doubts. \'93You don\'92t think Tony\'85 I slept like the dead that night we shared a room after the Vegas Show.\'94\
It was Kathy\'92s turn to become indignant. \'93Yet again with the dissing Chanta? He\'92d never be like that.\'94 Kathy really needed a patch.\
Deep in her heart, Sarah did know that. \'93So what now?\'94 she asked, though something in Kathy\'92s expression suggested she already had a plan.\
Kathy glanced back at the ultrasound screen. Normal. Comfortingly normal. Which, under the circumstances, made it feel almost suspicious.\
She moved to a covered apparatus in the corner and tugged the sheet back. To Sarah it looked like a large white M&M character that was reclining back at an angle and had its mouth open in a wide \'93O\'94.\
Kathy patted it. \'93This one, curiously, is called SARA\'97Superconducting Array for Reproductive Assessment.\'94\
Sarah shivered as she realised the open mouth was large enough to suck on her distended belly.\
Kathy wheeled it closer. \'93Nearly two hundred SQUID sensors,\'94 she explained.\
\'93I hope you\'92re not saying my baby is a squid.\'94 Sarah was equal parts joking and horrified.\
\'93No. SQUID: Superconducting Quantum Interference Device.\'94\
\'93Oh good, that makes me feel much better.\'94 Sarah wondered why medical equipment designers always took their aesthetic inspiration from Ulyssian House and Dungeon. The thing looked like a cross between an oversized egg cup and a Johnny Ive inspired torture device. \'93Is it safe?\'94\
\'93Oh, it\'92s got an app!\'94 Kathy effused and held up her tablet. \'93It\'92s completely passive\'97only detects, doesn\'92t emit. The magnetic fields generated by neural activity are incredibly weak\'97billionths of a tesla. But SARA can pick them up. If your baby\'92s brain is doing something extraordinary, this will show us.\'94\
\'93So, I\'85?\'94\
\'93Hop off the table,\'94 Kathy said happily. \'93You kneel on the pads there either side, and just let your belly\'97\'94\
\'93Flop in the M&M\'92s mouth.\'94\
\'93There you go,\'94 Kathy continued. \'93Let your arms drape over the runners here and your head rest on the padding between.\'94\
Sarah did as she was instructed. To her surprise, engaging with the contraption was almost comfortable.\
Kathy checked the tablet again, thumb hovering for a beat longer than it needed to. Then she activated the system.\
The attached display console erupted with data. Where the ultrasound had shown nothing unusual, SARA revealed waves of neural activity unlike anything Kathy had seen in the literature, or in practice, or in the kind of late-night rabbit holes you went down when you were trying not to think about your own life.\
Kathy took a breath and leaned closer to the console monitor. \'93These patterns\'85\'94\
Sarah\'92s mind\'92s eye was overcome with complex geometric forms\'97spirals and interconnected nodes that seemed to follow a mathematical logic. They pulsed with a purposeful rhythm, creating interference patterns that looked almost like language.\
Sarah gasped and pushed herself out of the contraption\'92s cradle, keeling over it with frozen knees and elbows. She blinked. Hard. And coughed.\
Kathy was at her side in an instant, hand hovering uselessly between help and panic. \'93Sarah. What happened? What did you feel?\'94\
\'93I just saw\'97while I was in that thing\'97the same patterns I\'92ve been dreaming of, only clearer and more vivid.\'94 Sarah swallowed and tasted gel and fear. \'93They\'92re like the scars in that bunker that Johnny blew up.\'94\
Kathy punched a few buttons and twiddled a dial, replaying the last run on the console monitor. \'93You mean, like this.\'94\
Sarah looked over her shoulder at the screen. The images were not as detailed as the flash she received. \'93Uh, yeah.\'94\
Kathy helped Sarah to stand and patted the examination table for her to sit.\
\'93I feel it, Kathy. Little Clinton. He\'92s not just developing. He\'92s\'85 preparing. For something.\'94\
With her hands gently on Sarah\'92s shoulders, she urged her to lie back.\
\'93What\'92s happening, Kathy?\'94\
\'93I don\'92t know,\'94 Kathy confessed. \'93All I can say is that Little Clinton is\'85 extraordinary.\'94\
\'93Is he going to be okay?\'94\
While Kathy wouldn\'92t buy a mother\'92s unscientific assessment of her baby possessing the ability to \'93prepare\'94\'97whatever that meant\'97the coherence of the readings she\'92d seen on the console monitor, as indecipherable as she found them, suggested health and vitality more than illness. \'93Yes, he\'92s going to be fine,\'94 she answered with more conviction than she felt. \'93He\'92s going to be more than fine.\'94 That felt truer.\
Panic set in for Sarah. She grabbed Kathy with claw-like hands. \'93Kathy, we can\'92t tell Eloise of this. No one can know,\'94 she pleaded. \'93He\'92s just a baby. Eloise will want to exploit him.\'94\
Kathy feared that may have been Eloise\'92s plan all along.\
\'93My baby is not a specimen for research,\'94 Sarah commanded.\
Kathy stared at the SARA readouts for a long time. The data were beautiful and terrifying.\
\'93I need people who see my child as a person, not a specimen.\'94 Sarah\'92s voice was fierce despite her exhaustion.\
Kathy swallowed. What these tests needed more than anything was more tests. And possibly a supercomputer. It was nothing more than an intuition, but Kathy wondered if she crunched the datapoints, they might flatten into the patterns Sarah reported seeing.\
Kathy flipped the machine off and wheeled it back into the corner. If Eloise cared to look, she\'92d see the equipment Kathy had requisitioned. She would be curious, she\'92d demand clear, objective, persuasive answers. Kathy would have to lie to her, and lie well.\
\'93Kathy?\'94 Sarah watched her friend\'92s mood turn solemn, watched her retrieve her handbag from the side-table and fossick around in its depths, watched her pull out a patch, peel off its protective layer, then apply it to her shoulder.\
\'93Tax it,\'94 Kathy said. \'93I can lie to the best of them.\'94
